#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9844-73f6-5e23-8163-b914b076bb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266554} {:answer/id #uuid "3c09471a-0067-5f14-90cd-cf33ee232ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266670} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba8357a-f4a7-53ad-b9c2-6266ec35f846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267096} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1e0ab1-f58e-5fcc-94e3-b0b2ec3950bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267985} {:answer/id #uuid "f71b468a-3aa2-52a6-b6a3-47a27498261d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ba8357a-f4a7-53ad-b9c2-6266ec35f846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267096} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8e533f-21a1-5986-ae72-7ac63280f4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05952a75-ee49-5c6e-985c-55f5554bd3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-135311" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dde5de-291d-5d7a-ab58-2b34e5fbdb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266188} {:answer/id #uuid "08d57faf-8683-5457-926a-17017f7019d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266225} {:answer/id #uuid "11eee0fb-37f6-56ae-ad49-4d70b6f72528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266292} {:answer/id #uuid "786a538d-7672-56a6-8d44-fe689a21bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267202} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e5b9b-1d4e-59df-8f41-441fa243cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a82e5b9b-1d4e-59df-8f41-441fa243cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267667} :answer-field/id #uuid "99885d5c-06cc-5a79-9a5e-f4b785659c96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cb13afc-7824-57e2-abcc-e880dfaafeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-135488" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$  is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0.5=1 \\\\ 0.3+0.7=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3368d843-8603-5c4c-b4cb-25b74f31633e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266593} {:answer/id #uuid "36ff86bd-1ede-55d5-a859-e58fb94f85e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266617} {:answer/id #uuid "71805fe1-5e3d-5b09-ac37-2600f1102822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267141} {:answer/id #uuid "7202d3e8-c960-597b-a8c2-21c29a071989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267144} {:answer/id #uuid "ee06a8e1-51fc-53ed-bd87-fae6a1608ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ff86bd-1ede-55d5-a859-e58fb94f85e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266617} :answer-field/id #uuid "223d714a-e779-5321-ae85-c5fc8fa4e232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139455" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02126ae9-f3b8-53eb-ad4f-409cfcff5fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161} {:answer/id #uuid "45ffec10-ec53-5719-87c3-efda8153a53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266769} {:answer/id #uuid "4d45cc36-3b81-57bd-a710-7ff416585a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266828} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b38899-fa5e-5f1d-84c8-4f2ef25127d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267653} {:answer/id #uuid "d485bcf4-5498-5b63-a595-81ab0e925eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ffec10-ec53-5719-87c3-efda8153a53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266769} :answer-field/id #uuid "4177a06e-6006-5e5c-be24-81a0aba0b4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80bdad61-ebf7-546e-ab4f-0e8486894231" :question/math-academy-id "q-139458" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b52156e-913a-5de2-a794-91999e64e3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269490} {:answer/id #uuid "398d644a-ad1c-5b35-ab05-7d0d212bd5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269792} {:answer/id #uuid "5f656659-28c8-5de3-ba39-06241a0c7984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270114} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeae5f3-7e46-54d5-a50f-b08ce65d16f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271059} {:answer/id #uuid "f68a27ff-793d-5e4f-bd44-233ef37fb985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b52156e-913a-5de2-a794-91999e64e3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269490} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0c2c23-b649-5839-b71c-7052c63f3cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a127128-cb22-56be-ba7d-5b1447ce81f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139957" :question/problem "Suppose $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a<0$\n- $b\\cdot a>0$\n- $b\\cdot a=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II and III are false. Simplifying the expression $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (a+b) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $b\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d184c8-0bd2-597e-9f52-d467a1379446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270741} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf57269-cf34-5bc0-87a7-29587dde8040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270933} {:answer/id #uuid "eba3d099-e4c6-5ebd-9f52-ee8c8c6e2d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff03a777-dcd9-5248-813a-477b910d2d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271498} {:answer/id #uuid "ff98c885-c7e6-5a89-827b-7c7ca0ba1eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf57269-cf34-5bc0-87a7-29587dde8040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270933} :answer-field/id #uuid "a04d5514-5b37-56fd-aa92-29d4ff83f34c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09aa5cfb-0ccb-582a-bfa8-b6151a1b73a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139979" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+3y=1 \\\\ 7x-2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $-3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nII. $3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c4fdf7-c46a-530c-a71c-14c56651b492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269718} {:answer/id #uuid "3c485c53-cb4c-5476-8b1c-aa0cd5cd9666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269819} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa0f0ae-b6e2-53a5-9c84-de5353f70624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270216} {:answer/id #uuid "bddefd59-382b-5e8e-ba2e-d6adc8315f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270944} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4d8ca4-c11a-5891-ad37-3bcc21d34a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aa0f0ae-b6e2-53a5-9c84-de5353f70624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9d4a45-bd99-5ff1-8095-ab37bfea3c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddc658b6-e0a7-590f-af72-c80671fc917f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139980" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y=-1 \\\\ kx+13y=-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- $-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dc653f0-653a-51c8-9dde-134e1ea1131e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269831} {:answer/id #uuid "63282b85-24f9-5737-88f6-e544d18bead1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270146} {:answer/id #uuid "d283f88f-ed57-5c59-963f-f49777d28ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271122} {:answer/id #uuid "db49a675-ce1e-57fa-a849-aff9de416a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271188} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb23ab9-9422-51b8-8613-a343d4227bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecb23ab9-9422-51b8-8613-a343d4227bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271348} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfceea30-a8b2-5f6c-aa6e-a6e0d02cdbfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- $A$ does not have identical rows\n- $A$ does not have proportional columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4277cba2-c049-5ea5-856e-2ff6513db5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269868} {:answer/id #uuid "72da4e3f-61a8-5e34-b36c-55d3e99b038d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270284} {:answer/id #uuid "9abb2b39-bec9-5be0-99bf-81a5af073417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270633} {:answer/id #uuid "9afef988-3992-5f75-bb57-47478f3265c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270637} {:answer/id #uuid "a39e8d77-7621-59cd-b046-86dcbd4ec894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39e8d77-7621-59cd-b046-86dcbd4ec894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270727} :answer-field/id #uuid "f058dbfa-a132-5778-8502-105f66b5f7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14e63fd3-62b7-575e-b489-4cda5c20ed53" :question/math-academy-id "q-140050" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has identical columns\n- $A$ has identical rows" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e440973-55fe-51fa-9da0-f4d52cf2e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269685} {:answer/id #uuid "6564e814-315d-5d94-906f-1b6ff52f2ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270172} {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270825} {:answer/id #uuid "cc38f4d0-c9ec-5bea-9900-3e87852c1726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271062} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8a718e-676e-5690-ab51-91d91cda239d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270825} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab21a93-10c6-5a81-9fe7-a6f973ee6db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cac76f2-abeb-58d4-87e9-5f3619bedd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140085" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dbab242-24fe-5c03-852d-2eb615913781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270370} {:answer/id #uuid "89b74ed6-8548-5297-8f9f-660681b98c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270460} {:answer/id #uuid "9de2dfb5-c1f9-5f6c-b906-4cc61c38d798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270667} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a2cdd4-2029-5723-81cc-f1c11dbb08be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271006} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dffa0e-a0ed-5ee7-ae33-b94be7866f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b74ed6-8548-5297-8f9f-660681b98c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b675c94-5368-5806-8976-ba02b13c17a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0e37cf1-8e7e-52da-9921-f2cfa10fc56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140434" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}$ and $g(t)=3{t}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u={t}^{3}+t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001c26e6-fb2a-5123-a23a-c6fe6c178c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269258} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc32ca-217a-5c44-ab30-9e62ad2048fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269830} {:answer/id #uuid "5313ba94-5a47-5645-9e76-9f46ce8302ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270006} {:answer/id #uuid "9d603cce-fb2b-52c8-bb52-08e08084f50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,$ $V$ and $W$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270662} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdc9516-9cc0-57a8-9a6c-f29d58f44264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcdc9516-9cc0-57a8-9a6c-f29d58f44264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270931} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce9eea1-f0fc-50cb-933d-e3ae585d23d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b412ef2-b86f-5688-ad69-1945a2870af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-140480" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0+3-3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0-3+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43749ce2-e780-5676-bb45-6925677458ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269877} {:answer/id #uuid "74320017-1113-5e96-92ae-f49ff4ac56dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,V,$ and $W$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270296} {:answer/id #uuid "b20691db-9c02-5118-962a-22f06eebd1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270862} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c50a9f-02a1-56cd-81ca-f7974fea67c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271028} {:answer/id #uuid "fdca36a0-0082-5d07-b30e-0c3eaadd6d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdca36a0-0082-5d07-b30e-0c3eaadd6d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c4dc67-f948-53ac-801a-382cfa00851a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d09c8cc-56af-54e4-b26f-33ef3a4b7c45" :question/math-academy-id "q-140483" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}+0-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4176e5a5-f3e3-5b6e-9a01-e183549a0659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269860} {:answer/id #uuid "4761f8a5-2ece-54ae-8bf9-29d721b95fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269916} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5f7f80-b91e-55cd-95cc-3b053fce1582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270485} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15f76f-cb2c-5ddf-9398-e0999218fa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271352} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a61a68-1fe2-5984-b5fb-d97021d24541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed15f76f-cb2c-5ddf-9398-e0999218fa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271352} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebc996f-c4f2-5d87-89ff-091ab407dc08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "762f53a4-6eba-533a-aefb-847dcb22ecb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140484" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6}{13}-\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=4+9=13\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2018f499-871d-52eb-9dcb-6eb74af91b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269536} {:answer/id #uuid "28183480-cfb9-5dd9-820e-7245cf6418ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269624} {:answer/id #uuid "988d29b3-3a9b-5bcf-a853-66f2587681a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270613} {:answer/id #uuid "a0144298-79ed-5a85-956d-a14aa98d90c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270691} {:answer/id #uuid "be2dc30f-2e10-5e59-921e-fc1967641251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988d29b3-3a9b-5bcf-a853-66f2587681a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270613} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d564bb-a546-5fa5-a0f0-f3c6a4a67e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0aa2e7ba-284c-52fb-a0ea-9a38be5541e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140500" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-(e-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-({e}^{-1}-e) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7e32e1-d561-5693-adb9-5951da0681df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270100} {:answer/id #uuid "6d40da93-f481-564b-96cd-9482db582939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270237} {:answer/id #uuid "8332a5db-afd8-508f-a213-afa6bb52b52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270403} {:answer/id #uuid "d1412055-1f36-50a9-869f-3b2d0276b8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271112} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a6b8f1-4197-52b7-bdc4-c12a32c65321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1412055-1f36-50a9-869f-3b2d0276b8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271112} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2dd264c-2679-51b0-aa2f-f68ee4606671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3d61daa-c943-5ecf-a98c-7f9bcd210491" :question/math-academy-id "q-140565" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n\n- If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- $Q-I$ is singular" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8f5c37-787f-5301-988e-881177dba05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269942} {:answer/id #uuid "70fd7dd5-e9f9-5e84-8182-d272b1a83d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270272} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec7d76-5885-5730-82c1-783eb5e8767a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270775} {:answer/id #uuid "a90a1400-8cf7-5327-84a6-b23eeef373c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270776} {:answer/id #uuid "bd969ae2-808e-586d-99a0-ec6d4ec6bd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70fd7dd5-e9f9-5e84-8182-d272b1a83d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270272} :answer-field/id #uuid "161805ab-30d2-5ed4-b289-e0037d8b336b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27c4d64e-3a6c-52f2-bb70-03acd0cf6bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140834" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cf5e4e-0cbf-5768-9b98-a3e3b69116cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269742} {:answer/id #uuid "4d383d45-4c02-5376-a1d2-94606118fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269964} {:answer/id #uuid "7caeac7e-597d-5341-8a6b-261132fa12f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270361} {:answer/id #uuid "c05018a0-89fa-5bed-b3a1-8ebd55b0f67b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270972} {:answer/id #uuid "ed384a5b-fa80-587a-85ae-ce6d0680f465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d383d45-4c02-5376-a1d2-94606118fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269964} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d37585f-1e97-5d22-a32b-6ec88d1ca95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d68a509c-d790-5483-ab3b-63aef29c3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140870" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ffeb00-a92e-56bf-93a8-fa4ad00e1bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269295} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfe8132-1976-54f4-a5ba-6797e3443c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269390} {:answer/id #uuid "27430119-3953-5959-8c3c-19cd82247a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269614} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea27a89-75a9-59bf-a4c5-de0126835372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269844} {:answer/id #uuid "b01b23bf-d234-5d0f-bc2c-5b3707b41d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ea27a89-75a9-59bf-a4c5-de0126835372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269844} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e0013a-858e-5dd4-84f3-c7915bebcebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7059aa8-79d3-5da6-b8bc-f64a765f9c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141014" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x<0 \\\\ -1, & x=0 \\\\ x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\frac{1}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04acee2f-7e36-55d8-af85-9d00b145e222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269311} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9b1282-6cc9-5d2c-bb98-b6960d4351da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269841} {:answer/id #uuid "4887b9da-4ae1-55e5-81c2-6c730fa8cf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269932} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba73fad-7a10-59ac-9d89-7542d2b369fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270357} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93b0ab-7385-52a6-9c9a-fdde1cb5b5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ba73fad-7a10-59ac-9d89-7542d2b369fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270357} :answer-field/id #uuid "a134005c-2718-5147-bc73-3c3851d6e560" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a7b393e-0e8e-512c-933e-c05c7c1e88c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141016" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x<\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 1, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 2x, & x>\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}h(x) & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ace00e-28b4-5eb3-a5ee-9b81b0aad320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270152} {:answer/id #uuid "a59765a1-9493-5117-8263-3fd722a0b90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270751} {:answer/id #uuid "bee5f5db-b60e-57c9-a3e0-66019d07b791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270957} {:answer/id #uuid "d038cee2-720b-59d0-b5b4-7548d4d8268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271099} {:answer/id #uuid "d8951eb1-320f-5122-be61-f0267f4479bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d038cee2-720b-59d0-b5b4-7548d4d8268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271099} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3941ee1-c43e-57bf-a530-dfdd0c1a87d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3376f0ce-d656-5952-8ec5-482d4a788f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-141017" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x<-1 \\\\ 1, & x=-1 \\\\ x+2, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}h(x) & =2(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-2+1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}h(x) & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a4ad32-a873-582b-96ae-ed02f26f7e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269609} {:answer/id #uuid "6514b6a0-1a44-54a4-a5cf-1f9f398648a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270169} {:answer/id #uuid "668a240d-1631-510d-a9b4-c9d7bbdd0f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270179} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea0306-d45d-5d31-b36c-d44f29d74ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270348} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2dadc1-bee4-5e05-a544-04e4b92d8545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a4ad32-a873-582b-96ae-ed02f26f7e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269609} :answer-field/id #uuid "c371ee98-f872-5961-b48b-e16e995f5c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a9082ef-b5c5-508a-ad30-c16427769a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141209" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}-8}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32879a09-f2f5-5307-852d-bdcc80ac28fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269732} {:answer/id #uuid "5193b80b-aad5-55dd-8026-5fcce1aaa215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269999} {:answer/id #uuid "7321fe4c-6365-5e6c-9321-5a1e32c712c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270288} {:answer/id #uuid "e05f83bc-9cbf-50fa-8e83-a22bd0a5bf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271240} {:answer/id #uuid "efacb9a9-087a-548a-a494-9ae0ca07c85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05f83bc-9cbf-50fa-8e83-a22bd0a5bf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b230196c-4db7-51b1-b127-98b23dc5d317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c6c3c56-7a5d-5137-ba4b-48f3b706f731" :question/math-academy-id "q-141212" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0ef079-5f06-5e7c-adef-068bae5538ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269519} {:answer/id #uuid "357e3f3b-92ad-5333-b803-0f7d4d33b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269758} {:answer/id #uuid "55026d12-8574-5fbe-8c07-f7e5bd6d4ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270025} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc10ea1-5dc4-5c43-8e23-bce8bb264142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271090} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0eda8-710f-5ddd-b5e0-4b4ab8b7f985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357e3f3b-92ad-5333-b803-0f7d4d33b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269758} :answer-field/id #uuid "57dc7a29-f195-55f8-9696-8efd8402e367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c19bd8b-6180-555e-84ee-4d1df41e83f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141218" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} {:answer/id #uuid "46da9874-30bc-583c-a863-5aae9560a9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269913} {:answer/id #uuid "59a1700f-ccf6-5e35-9e43-a7880fc832ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270065} {:answer/id #uuid "75df7d2e-780f-56cd-92ad-5720fda4a706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270310} {:answer/id #uuid "c456bd0a-afad-5d98-8f69-257b595ed130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} :answer-field/id #uuid "903f2292-1c52-539d-84aa-b1a6a93b0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n\nII. $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023f4ad7-4e27-58df-b9cf-7d7dff9ec1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269287} {:answer/id #uuid "67f707f9-a11f-5cd5-b66d-da214b7f66c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270192} {:answer/id #uuid "d00b4f12-2503-53b5-ad0e-43dad5b4ff65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271096} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e53e33-d114-5a0d-8f9d-97c29350e249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271178} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc39fd6-b0b1-5463-86d7-17f95b8035dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00b4f12-2503-53b5-ad0e-43dad5b4ff65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271096} :answer-field/id #uuid "2352a387-0098-58a2-9edc-386e6c666edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6242aae-9378-55e8-b137-ddfe7f38d97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141236" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576103fa-ed39-5cc1-b3dd-aa1c41c6e6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270040} {:answer/id #uuid "850b62e1-b775-571c-9058-04cc09e464b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270419} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d1b938-e87f-5462-aecb-d2ec3dd3795d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270910} {:answer/id #uuid "d48c0194-9812-5494-b598-95ff7ec91143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271137} {:answer/id #uuid "d736a809-e9dd-5b1c-8eab-1c5a18819085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "850b62e1-b775-571c-9058-04cc09e464b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270419} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c7e2f7-2db6-5d5d-9fb1-1b41e12897f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3f0c75-73fd-5349-840a-8899dcbd367a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141240" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$\n- $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e78141c-5b1b-5383-9436-dd7961708aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269398} {:answer/id #uuid "377e209c-dca0-560e-b0e9-3dd954d6f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269771} {:answer/id #uuid "b9af0254-5aa7-512b-b479-10e85a3c90b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270908} {:answer/id #uuid "eba4ea75-16bf-56cf-b877-0a5e9c421d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271340} {:answer/id #uuid "f61c3215-545e-5c9e-9d54-4f213478cea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e78141c-5b1b-5383-9436-dd7961708aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269398} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ded575-1ef1-5611-9129-6853eed9845c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e46443-6b0b-52be-80af-856b64b460d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141245" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$\n- $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$\n- $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429117a3-6e54-516f-90dc-8b3c46852d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269870} {:answer/id #uuid "540ff262-b6d8-55d6-bdff-7a8f7374caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270017} {:answer/id #uuid "953e5319-0e76-5d91-be9e-97acbe17adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270577} {:answer/id #uuid "c637029f-ce11-54ac-92f8-b41a82831cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271015} {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f70c062-2c20-514b-a050-6ed7cd19ecf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141495" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa63a2-fcd6-59c7-b8d5-c7ae94cea07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269357} {:answer/id #uuid "222c1fca-f9be-5ece-b1ab-40086be18680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269563} {:answer/id #uuid "a1454b89-eae8-5cc9-b299-541dbb47ae01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270698} {:answer/id #uuid "cebbe3cb-717f-56f5-b65c-230ba3342707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271082} {:answer/id #uuid "f48fdfad-2d33-560f-be43-a5d4c13ef17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222c1fca-f9be-5ece-b1ab-40086be18680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269563} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf7ad5e-33c5-5362-84f5-c267e5402f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4507db3-2027-5030-88fd-955381171b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-141569" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{nullity}(T)=4,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4}$\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b3cb5a-341a-59e5-a715-d12977c5bed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269708} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe66b4f-fc34-5672-a3db-4734e66a55a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270116} {:answer/id #uuid "96a19ab7-cc61-5293-86a8-bafa6581e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270594} {:answer/id #uuid "cb05382b-ecd7-547d-89c0-4aef033b29c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271053} {:answer/id #uuid "da7eed7b-ee2d-5936-b766-061ccc59fa6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a19ab7-cc61-5293-86a8-bafa6581e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270594} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ad95d2-baf4-5b0d-81cc-ba23c5a39a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2805dfb0-8d9a-5775-8051-a005b74b03a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141584" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $T$ is not a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093de82c-3c10-509e-a115-10c02890e9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269353} {:answer/id #uuid "138068fd-1aed-52e6-ac5f-add922c4dca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269443} {:answer/id #uuid "3638d5d5-8aa0-56ce-b0f5-e70107346880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269762} {:answer/id #uuid "f79af8a6-d754-5246-a9ad-95b33a49ce2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271441} {:answer/id #uuid "fdae7495-8e53-5c68-9f1d-812a9ee8c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdae7495-8e53-5c68-9f1d-812a9ee8c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271490} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd64f202-1cd1-5be2-bcac-f3c8e7039c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bb3b4f0-a153-5b1f-93aa-f136a6132e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-141751" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d5ea7e13fa03ac553893aa78aa7ee32ac7b67f2eb19dff32228d53bf2010aa2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5177ea3a-9272-590f-8f16-d605b524db47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269998} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a782a-c7a0-5f39-9b30-7e1f9ae49e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270090} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2623b-ee3b-5cc2-863d-41dcce18a077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270363} {:answer/id #uuid "a640d8d9-7217-514b-aa3a-b01bab9dbd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270757} {:answer/id #uuid "be7733e3-82d9-51c4-9a92-5cf1de7b00b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2623b-ee3b-5cc2-863d-41dcce18a077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270363} :answer-field/id #uuid "6372289f-4413-56d4-a466-f00fa976e952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13227862-4183-5d5b-b484-99ef3e29fd6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141754" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2b904027ec6319fe5857f81089469568322d148e49cdf31bb20c07f1f31daf4.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nAll graphs I, II, and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c27800-0300-5249-b4a0-b6e008f8e83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269558} {:answer/id #uuid "44d71b0a-4454-5106-ad0c-ed629eabb1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269900} {:answer/id #uuid "708d14c2-579e-5e65-9cd2-447b05f510fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270265} {:answer/id #uuid "a29c9c7d-1f79-5b6d-b1d6-1b0537c578cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270720} {:answer/id #uuid "f7830376-f651-56ea-b56d-c7df40cee1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d71b0a-4454-5106-ad0c-ed629eabb1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269900} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f46adb-5d16-505a-be52-2235f2f08ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c320bbb-0961-56b0-99e4-beaa75aa8f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-142358" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is  invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ccbab42-afa2-5f66-ac0e-c308c66e8202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269668} {:answer/id #uuid "82c7cc8b-3d11-56b8-a528-bbb1a33f1d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270399} {:answer/id #uuid "952d8149-19cf-533c-af96-872b8df55506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270576} {:answer/id #uuid "d8826fb6-eeca-5b0d-8d8b-f2147e992c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d12440-aa2a-5690-b9a2-07e79a593ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d12440-aa2a-5690-b9a2-07e79a593ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271262} :answer-field/id #uuid "10bb68a7-51a0-5f0c-b2b1-a21badc3475a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142475" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0895ab25-c3a3-5de5-8c7f-b29240d396fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269347} {:answer/id #uuid "70dbe989-e5af-54e0-b64e-b2b8f149ec2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270271} {:answer/id #uuid "8abda789-acc5-5411-8952-794fc73da646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270468} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e8d21b-4e62-5248-a3d7-8afaad5786f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270745} {:answer/id #uuid "be53ddb2-3789-59ea-8a22-87a097d55924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0895ab25-c3a3-5de5-8c7f-b29240d396fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269347} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe26b88-241f-530c-8ccf-982cdb7e7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7036dc73-9ca4-59f2-b983-11eaacfb5a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142476" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}$\n\nIII. $A$ has $8$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times $\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca6964b-f001-50a6-962e-37568669ccd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272499} {:answer/id #uuid "38adbd90-9f47-5cfa-b9c1-7dcb290c0580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272883} {:answer/id #uuid "759af798-3374-5082-9e33-2c081c92dd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273389} {:answer/id #uuid "c73c83bf-34b1-563e-83a1-c83796722f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274119} {:answer/id #uuid "fa69b46f-7365-5071-aefe-c9610d1635b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73c83bf-34b1-563e-83a1-c83796722f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274119} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c232438-e4d9-548e-93c1-3bdeab4b5cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6378e369-5031-5af4-8366-576c75d82fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143001" :question/problem "![](images/67/67706db50684aef52269d20065caa21e4301834835e10e1fd3e456646ae2b32e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DF}?$\n\nI. $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n\nII. $\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n\nIII. $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB})+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB} & =\\overset{\\to }{FB}+\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f4640-df8c-5f58-8473-a42a9d3b80e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272417} {:answer/id #uuid "4dffa4f0-5259-5130-adb8-cb6e99c26fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273042} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2716f3-ecca-55e3-979f-f0158825f290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273160} {:answer/id #uuid "c042c4d0-435e-5e30-98e5-20c5ed9387ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274051} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb3ddd5-6976-5815-aaaa-8715e648bc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c042c4d0-435e-5e30-98e5-20c5ed9387ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274051} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ed2a0e-62c9-5160-8b40-423a8e0dfc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32cdac5a-ca17-59c1-8641-8b3b47e09944" :question/math-academy-id "q-143006" :question/problem "![](images/15/15f4bd7f8ecf8f61ce84858e33cdfa468b0de360fac19b0713b6b2dbc4af8d2e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF}=\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF} & =\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{FA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BF}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+(\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}) \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BE}+\\overset{\\to }{ED})+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025d6b48-01e5-5f01-8d65-c71247ac2c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272402} {:answer/id #uuid "33299f41-5d8f-547d-ad5c-48ed2d2cc7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272842} {:answer/id #uuid "4db325db-0d80-5c94-8d47-63cd60da22e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273039} {:answer/id #uuid "b708c468-1a5f-5884-843d-d4de1dfc18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273974} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc201f5-22fa-5ca2-81f0-aa15cf93f039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b708c468-1a5f-5884-843d-d4de1dfc18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273974} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc06cb9-a10b-5ac4-80d0-be2806f417c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a347e7c2-3db8-5058-926f-eeec20d91744" :question/math-academy-id "q-143331" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=1)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb511a6-9e3f-5fab-80ba-b266dbff4317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272500} {:answer/id #uuid "0f80c1c4-b8b0-5d20-8f58-ee939af16ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272524} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4845ad-be2d-5904-9060-d4ff681ee8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273616} {:answer/id #uuid "baf4e5bc-43d2-59fe-b996-7b9f861daebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274009} {:answer/id #uuid "fda572d8-a7cb-50d0-afbf-d1498df635a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb511a6-