and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.05.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.3\\cdot 0.65=0.195.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4360e-11aa-50a5-8dca-4dea8a8fdd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272992} {:answer/id #uuid "892b0517-6002-5679-8747-3e18957a3c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273568} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d73e2e-3487-5a60-a79c-f7913c20e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274069} {:answer/id #uuid "ca570e3b-a84a-5db7-a59c-54a705149244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274144} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f2b8dc-2166-5acf-96d8-0e209e971eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892b0517-6002-5679-8747-3e18957a3c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8185736-7186-5fec-abfc-3ab0eb208611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2475e8ae-4517-52f5-bd41-fe3d91352945" :question/math-academy-id "q-143680" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89bddea6-bbe2-51b8-adf4-de306f1c6836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273576} {:answer/id #uuid "8e52a1ab-35d5-5c3f-8591-929097128649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273617} {:answer/id #uuid "94a7a522-7254-5d79-914a-36556eb0b65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273682} {:answer/id #uuid "b22de866-0abf-5b09-a709-899483e618d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273926} {:answer/id #uuid "ea17a99a-d10e-5140-b7fb-13d127018d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22de866-0abf-5b09-a709-899483e618d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273926} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fbf250-71a6-5396-96bb-258f31270702" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62d0b863-0ebd-5fe2-bf2e-b910931d25fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143865" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions is true?\n\nI. $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$\n\nIII. $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition I is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition II is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.4+0.3+0.2+0.1=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions I and II, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e902443-9355-50ce-8de1-debd70c8598d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272516} {:answer/id #uuid "52ba22d1-99d1-54d0-9257-31ef1b341758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273091} {:answer/id #uuid "6af8ca05-f0a1-5735-af6c-6ac5a6c38642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273309} {:answer/id #uuid "9355ff7b-7286-587b-9a49-30b401f5cc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273668} {:answer/id #uuid "bcfdbafb-392e-5335-9f27-fdf0786d2811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcfdbafb-392e-5335-9f27-fdf0786d2811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274021} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda186df-8fcf-599c-9d99-b672e70220eb" :answer-field/key "selection" 